            },
            "title": "Synthesis"
          },
          {
            "text": {
              "status": "generated",
              "div": "<div xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\">Dear colleague, I am writing to inform you about the recent hospital admission of your patient, a 37-year-old male, who presented on 22/01/1990 with acute right lower quadrant abdominal pain, nausea, and low-grade fever. Clinical examination revealed rebound tenderness at McBurney's point. Laboratory investigations confirmed elevated inflammatory markers, and abdominal ultrasound supported the diagnosis of acute appendicitis. The patient was kept nil by mouth, commenced on intravenous fluids and antibiotics, and monitored closely. Given the clinical picture, a laparoscopic appendectomy was performed without intraoperative complications. The postoperative course was uneventful. Wound care was provided, and the patient mobilised well. He was discharged on 24/01/1990 in good general condition with oral analgesia and wound care instructions. Please arrange follow-up in approximately two weeks. I remain available for any further queries.</div>"
            },
